  Now the five attack questions:

  **(1) Copy-satisfiability — stub entry with five fake rows and five matching lane-authored SOURCES rows.**

  The counter reads only lane-authored bytes (entry + SOURCES + README coverage map). A stub `client-trust-gates-round68.md` containing five fabricated rows, plus five fabricated SOURCES rows S###, satisfies every byte-local conjunct: entry exists, table rows with quote cells, ids resolve to `status mined` rows, anchors match, coverage-map row indexed, verified-at/recheck present. N = 5, exit 0 — with zero live pages ever opened. This is copy-satisfiable *by construction* because the entry cites itself.

  Is this a defect or an accepted bound? The plan's Bound paragraph names this: "Documentary evidence, not observed runtime behaviour" — QUALITY Q33. So the contract knows. BUT — the critical question: does the frozen predicate's `--fetch` arm actually close this hole? Only if:
  - `--fetch` derives its quotes from the landed entry/SOURCES bytes at the rev (not hardcoded constants in the counter);
  - `--fetch` failure exits nonzero and is actually measured as a conjunct of the claim (the frozen predicate lists it as a separate line, but nothing in the contract says `--fetch` must run against the same landed rev's bytes, or that its failure blocks the claim).

  Attack A1 (HIGH): the frozen predicate never pins where `--fetch` gets its five quotes. If the counter hardcodes five quote strings as module constants, `--fetch` proves the constants exist on live pages while the landed entry can cite five *different* (fabricated) quotes. `--rev <rev>` never compares the entry's quotes to the live pages, so the stub world passes `--rev` 5-of-5 + base control + (constants-verified) `--fetch`. Fix: contract must state `--fetch` extracts each quote from the measured rev's entry/SOURCES bytes and fails (nonzero) when any quote is absent; and the claim's receipt must run `--fetch` at the landed rev.

  Evidence: contract text line 49 `--fetch finds each of the 5 located quotes on its live page` — "the 5 located quotes" is not bound to the entry's table or to the rev; contrast r67 counter which was fully rev-scoped (`load_blobs` reads only committed bytes). Also note: `--fetch` takes no `--rev` argument in the frozen predicate lines, so its rev-scoping is undefined by the contract itself.

  **(2) Does `--fetch` discriminate — nav menu / 404-page quote.**

  Attack A2 (HIGH): substring matching on live HTML accepts any occurrence, including navigation menus, breadcrumbs, footers, cookie banners, and 404/soft-404 pages. Concrete case: a quote of ≤25 words that includes generic client vocabulary ("skills", "untrusted", "instructions") will commonly appear in a sidebar/nav. Worse: many sites serve a soft-404 (HTTP 200 + "page not found" template) — if the fetcher checks only HTTP status loosely or follows redirects to a generic error page whose template contains boilerplate, a quote that appears in the boilerplate of *any* page of the domain would pass. Conversely, a wrong quote on a real page passes if it appears anywhere on the page (e.g., in the "related articles" list). The contract says only "finds each of the 5 located quotes on its live page" — no pinning of: URL equality (row's canonical URL vs. fetched URL after redirects), non-404 status, or occurrence outside chrome. Fix: contract must pin (a) fetch the exact canonical URL from the SOURCES row's source cell, (b) require HTTP 200 (and optionally reject obvious soft-404 markers), (c) case-sensitive verbatim substring match of the quote as written in the anchor cell, (d) exit 1 (content verdict) when a quote is absent on a 200 page, exit 2 on network/environment failure — and the two classes must be distinguishable in output.

